    "stdout_excerpt": "COUNTERFACT_JSON={\"expected\": {\"invalid\": [], \"missing\": [], \"refusal_class\": \"TmxProviderError\", \"refusal_code\": \"TMX_BAD_ROW\", \"rejected\": [], \"status\": null}, \"observed\": {\"invalid\": [], \"missing\": [], \"refusal_class\": \"TmxProviderError\", \"refusal_code\": \"TMX_BAD_ROW\", \"rejected\": [], \"status\": null}, \"reason\": \"the refusal is raised by CompanyNewsFetcher.transform_data in opendata/data/providers/tmx/models/fetchers_w33_graphql.py, at the isinstance(newsid, str) guard on the article id -- the site this leg's traceback ends in. tmx_company_news files its natural_key as url + date and the selection publishes no url: the mapper forms https://money.tmx.com/quote/<SYMBOL>/news/<newsid>, the address TMX's own UI uses (OpenBB openbb_tmx/models/company_news.py:113). Setting newsid to null is the tamper that shows the guard is load-bearing rather than decorative: format would happily render .../news/None, headline, datetime, source and summary all stay valid, and the leg would return two frozen contract rows whose keys name an article that does not exist. Nothing else on the path can catch it -- list_rows sees two well-formed dicts and the reply length equals the limit the request published; untampered control produced 2 row(s) through the same seam\"}\nVERDICT=PASS\n"
  },
